Three Pointers:
Three Pointers is an extension of the two-pointers technique where we use three indices to solve problems more efficiently.
It is commonly used when tracking three positions, partitioning arrays, or finding triplets.

When do we use Three Pointers?
3Sum (find triplets with a target sum)
Sort Colors (Dutch National Flag)
Merge or partition arrays
Problems involving three groups or three positions

Types
1. Fixed + Two Pointers
Fix one element, then use two pointers to find the remaining two.
Example: 3Sum
Time Complexity: O(n²)

2. Three Moving Pointers (Partitioning)
Three pointers (low, mid, high) divide the array into sections.
Example: Sort Colors (Dutch National Flag)
Time Complexity: O(n)

Interview Answer :

Three Pointers is a technique that uses three indices to solve problems efficiently. 
It is mainly used for triplet-sum problems and array partitioning. 
The two common patterns are Fixed + Two Pointers (used in 3Sum) and Three Moving Pointers (used in Sort Colors/Dutch National Flag).
